{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-49003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/03b4d6fea89eb695e0919aed4a82b1d25ff5e296338381192b5236f5295bef12.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)<0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-49003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We begin by noticing that at $x=b$ the graph has a corner point, which implies that the derivative doesn't exist there.\n\nThe derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is negative when $f(x)$ is decreasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=c$ and $x=e$ are on decreasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/f33639f6a54a2e0f35c52b684356ed832047ee538b8651e9ca9f75a542d366a9.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=c$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=c$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-49003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d92626a61642c5e6c8d789f38d6826566951f6bcd8a1712df62f19eb31a64fea"} {"question" "q-49003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-49003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=a"] ["math" "x=b"] ["math" "x=c"] ["math" "x=d"] ["math" "x=e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-49003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=c"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-51432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/0c8969fae3e21c8e5f5019509636e2307211921d742e6f067d9ee1c50499541b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-51432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upwards like a smile ($⌣$) over the intervals $(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/ae9ce6ca2597a4d5d1ca150c1e97d7c7a0a35f59fe5d6421b75203c6882d2052.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-51432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d92626a61642c5e6c8d789f38d6826566951f6bcd8a1712df62f19eb31a64fea"} {"question" "q-51432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-51432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,1)$ only"] ["text" "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3)$"] ["text" "$(-3,-1)$ only"] ["text" "$(-3,0)$ only"] ["text" "$(1,3)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-51432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-113899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/42fd32f22d25ed1d26d48ca8d4f1f5f454bcca67c68febaec066fa3b8bde0783.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* ${f}^{′}(x)<0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-113899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We begin by noticing that at $x=a$ the graph has a corner point, which implies that the derivative doesn't exist there.\n\nThe derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is negative when $f(x)$ is decreasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=c$ are on decreasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/f6e6c7b869268eba113690776b0aa07713ff12adb675e22e11406c4a13f0e7b2.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=c,$ the graph is concave down ($⌢$) at $x=c$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-113899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Cour