hen the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downwards like a frown ($⌢$) over the intervals $(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/ce57fc818eebd0cc5007e80a54b49d613eeb2823eefc98e946862ff9f7b68e0b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-141937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d92626a61642c5e6c8d789f38d6826566951f6bcd8a1712df62f19eb31a64fea"} {"question" "q-141937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-141937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,0)$ only"] ["text" "$(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$"] ["text" "$(-2,-1)$ only"] ["text" "$(-3,-2)$ and $(-1,0)$"] ["text" "$(-3,-2)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-141937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-142065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/de53aa4414f0c535562e0464b773928e505a98bea01afe5a12b2ec9b154b0b59.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)=0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-142065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=d$ are stationary points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/c7a00e3b25a9fc1a9a589234d0beb421330b58cfe69d3e745f432c3f9be09485.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=d,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=d$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/state.json" "file_sha256" "947690bafe5a14f377227a2925094d59f4ac83fae69744f9f119dce99339b88e"} {"question" "q-142065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mat